WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:07.999
In a world built upon trade and commerce,
we assign value to the items, locations, and

00:00:07.999 --> 00:00:13.090
experiences in our lives via their monetary
cost—which can be affected in any number

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.230
of ways.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.720
Supply and demand, for instance, not to mention
rarity.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.300
In other words, the more valuable we consider
something to be, the more we are willing to

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.520
pay for it.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.380
And if you have a lot of money to throw around,
there are plenty of ways to flex your wealth

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.949
for yourself or for others if you just know
where to look.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:40.989
To get you started, let’s check out nine
of the most expensive things in the world.

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:48.309
We start with the world’s most expensive
car: the 1962 Ferrari 250 GTO; a classic car

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:49.780
enthusiast’s dream come true.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:55.859
A gorgeous two-seater, with plenty of horsepower
thanks to the 3.0-litre V12 engine rumbling

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:59.730
beneath the hood, it’s going to get you
where you’re going with all the style and

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.600
poise that’s made Ferrari famous before
you can say “speeding ticket”.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.240
Though it makes a bold statement on the motorways,
its origins lie in secrecy—behind closed

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.240
doors.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.320
In 1961, after learning of the shiny new GT
that Jaguar had introduced to the racing world,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.650
Enzo Ferrari tasked one of his top engineers
with Ferrari’s response.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:28.460
The engineer, Giotto Bizzarrini, assembled
a small group of dependable men and set to

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:34.399
work—drawing up schematics for what would
become the 250 GTO after several months of

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:35.820
grueling work.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.659
It was to be faster, lighter, and more powerful
than the flagship Ferraris of the time—and

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:47.259
much to Enzo’s relief, early tests showed
that Bizzarrini’s design passed with flying

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:48.259
colors.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:54.850
One model, custom made for the famous British
racecar driver Stirling Moss, recently sold

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:02.170
for just over $38 million US dollars at auction,
setting a world record for the most money

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.219
ever to change hands in the sale of a car.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:10.000
Though this particular car has seen a great
deal of wear and tear—having been raced,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.830
crashed, and repaired many times over its
lifetime—this firecracker of a Ferrari saw

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:22.820
bidding begin at no less than $11 million
dollars before reaching the historic record.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:28.610
For true car enthusiasts, every car has a
story—age and dents included.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:34.490
Holding the keys to your own little portion
of car racing history is certainly a priceless

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.980
experience.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.990
When it comes to jewelry, diamonds are the
gem of choice to set in rings, necklaces,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:40.990
and earrings.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:46.280
Of the diamond family, the type best known
by the public is the relatively common white

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.280
diamond.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.790
But diamonds actually come in a wide variety
of colours, and not all the colours are synthetically

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.060
produced.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:00.160
Among the crowd favorite gemstones, authentic
pink diamonds are the rarest and most valuable

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.540
of them all.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:07.740
So it should come as no surprise that the
monstrous 59.6 carat “Pink Star” diamond

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.140
is currently the most expensive gemstone in
the world.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:18.200
The “Pink Star” went to auction in 2014,
prompting diamond cutter Isaac Wolf to place

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:26.020
a bid for a remarkable $83.2 million USD—shutting
out all rival bidders in the process.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:32.160
He even renamed the gem to the “Pink Dream”
while the bid was in progress, seemingly ready

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.520
to make his own dreams come true.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.410
But in the end, it seems he may have only
been interested in the prestige that comes

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:44.540
with making such an offer, as he never actually
finalized payment for the gem.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:49.090
Of course, dealing in such vast amounts of
money is often a grand dance between would-be

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.950
buyers and the investors backing them.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.520
Perhaps there was a bit of a mix-up at the
bank—you’ve got to be careful counting

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.490
so many zeroes.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.140
After Isaac defaulted on payment for the gem,
it was placed back in the care of Sotheby’s—the

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.380
world leader in luxury items and auctions.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:11.870
For now, it remains in their possession, revalued
at a more affordable $72 million dollars,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:17.250
though that is hardly the upper limit due
to the rarity and unusual size of the gem.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.820
At that price, it’s simply a steal.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.890
Best to purchase it before it goes back to
auction.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.180
Our homes serve many purposes.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.880
They are our castles, our sanctums, our refuges
from the hustle and bustle of the outside

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:32.880
world.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.070
So it’s no small wonder that our homes say
a lot about who we are, and what we value

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.070
most.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.910
For billionaire Mukesh Ambani, the wealthiest
man in India, his home represents his vast

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.780
wealth —and it’s appropriately awesome
in execution.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:55.230
He named the towering spire Antilia after
a mysterious and possibly mythological island

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:56.800
in the Atlantic Ocean.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.090
Clearly, Mukesh is not one for subtlety—the
$1 billion home looks every bit as legendary

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:09.870
as its namesake, and bears the unique honor
of being the most expensive home in the world.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:17.300
Amtilia has a total of 27 stories, six of
which comprise a subterranean parking garage,

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:23.889
one floor which is devoted entirely to health
and wellness, four floors of lavish open-air

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:30.370
gardens, one for a cinema, six residential
floors for guests and family, and three floors

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:35.120
set aside strictly for maintenance purposes
to keep everything running smoothly.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.680
And of course, no billionaire chateau would
be complete without its very own dedicated

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.530
staff; nearly 600 people see to every nook
and cranny of this monolithic dream home.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:52.050
The expenses might seem staggering—just
imagine his monthly utility bills—but for

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:57.910
a man with a net worth of over $27 billion
dollars, the effect such a home has on his

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:02.040
finances are likely little more than a drop
in the ocean.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:08.020
The whole building is reinforced against a
variety of disasters, both natural and potentially

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.130
malicious in nature.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:15.800
Antilia is built to stand tall in the face
of magnitude 8 earthquakes, and can even withstand

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.750
military grade explosions.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:24.000
But all things considered, Mr. Ambani’s
magnificent domicile only cost an estimated

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.780
$77 million to build.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:33.560
Yes, that’s a $923 million dollar return
on investment—though he shows no indication

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.700
that he would be interested in selling.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:40.480
For now, it looks like if you want your very
own tower of extravagance, you’ll have to

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.140
rustle up $77 million and build your own.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:49.780
Hollywood is no stranger to multimillion dollar
productions, but every few years, a special

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.580
sort of film comes along that ups the ante.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:57.340
The Pirates of the Caribbean films were smash
hits at the box office, so for the ongoing

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.860
adventures of the swashbuckling Captain Jack
Sparrow it’s hardly a surprise that Disney

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.690
spared no expense.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:08.330
The fourth film in the series, Pirates of
the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides, managed

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:15.210
to rack up an impressive $410.6 million in
costs before adjustment for tax credits and

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:20.500
other assistance—making it the most expensive
production in cinema to date.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:25.910
Cameras rolled in exotic locales across the
globe to set all manner of piratical excitement

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:31.050
to celluloid film, even filming some scenes
in jolly olde England.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.980
In addition to being a fantastic country all
around, it’s likely that the producers were

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:41.520
interested in the British tax credit offered
to films costing more than $34.1 million dollars—just

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.530
one of many ways Disney executives sought
to soften the blow of the bottom line.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:51.080
Disney banked a lot on the captain and his
crew, and luckily for the House of Mouse their

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:52.880
gamble paid off.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.190
Worldwide takes for the film sailed straight
past the $1 billion mark, as the captain and

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:00.840
his crew made their way to greener waters.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.800
I’m no mathematician, but that sounds like
a resounding financial success to me.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.400
With a fifth film on the horizon, will the
pirates be able to plunder the box office

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.400
again?

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.840
I suppose we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:17.380
Occasionally, we put our collective funds
toward the betterment of all mankind.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:24.270
The International Space Station, sitting in
low Earth orbit 330 kilometers above the surface

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:30.910
of our planet, represents the combined resources
and ingenuity of five major space agencies

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.260
across the world.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:37.560
The total cost of $100 billion dollars has
been shared among all nations contributing

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:44.070
to the project since its inception, making
it the most expensive manmade object we have

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.070
today.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:51.589
The purpose of the ISS is to provide a semi-permanent
orbital lab, where humankind can perform scientific

00:08:51.589 --> 00:08:56.880
experiments that take advantage of the unique
interactions of gravity, temperature, and

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:02.809
pressure in space, performing many tests which
would be simply impossible otherwise.

00:09:02.809 --> 00:09:08.170
Astronauts also have the privilege of testing
cutting-edge industrial materials and innovative

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:13.709
communications technology, as well as gathering
data for medical research on the side.

00:09:13.709 --> 00:09:18.720
Despite what you might think, it isn’t all
floaty fun and games in space—there’s

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.279
actually an awful lot of work that gets done.

00:09:21.279 --> 00:09:26.930
The ISS was granted a recent extension of
its life cycle, now projected to last through

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:32.839
2024, it pays to look to the future when dealing
with space.

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:41.100
Long term, the Russian Roscosmos and US based
NASA are drawing up plans for a new station,

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.520
to be constructed after the ISS reaches its
end-of-life.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.990
What comes after that is anyone’s guess;
but if Star Trek has taught us anything, it’s

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:56.009
that one of humanity’s noblest aspirations
is to boldly go where no one has gone before.

00:09:56.009 --> 00:09:57.879
And then make friends with aliens.

00:09:57.879 --> 00:10:03.050
Regardless of the cost we assigned to them,
books are one of the most valuable commodities

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:04.119
we have.

00:10:04.119 --> 00:10:10.190
They provide the opportunity to reach across
time and space, sharing information, concepts,

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:14.069
and ideas with others of our time and generations
yet to come.

00:10:14.069 --> 00:10:18.569
And of all the influential figures throughout
history whose thoughts might be worth reading,

00:10:18.569 --> 00:10:23.619
the great Leonardo Da Vinci would doubtlessly
rank quite highly.

00:10:23.619 --> 00:10:29.639
One of his compiled manuscripts, the Codex
Leicester, sold at auction in 1994 to Microsoft

00:10:29.639 --> 00:10:36.819
cofounder Bill Gates for over $30 million—the
greatest amount of money to ever change hands

00:10:36.819 --> 00:10:38.929
in the sale of a book.

00:10:38.929 --> 00:10:43.260
Also known as the “Codex Hammer” after
the previous owner of the manuscript, the

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:49.529
Codex Leicester was written by Da Vinci over
a period of time between 1506 and 1513.

00:10:49.529 --> 00:10:55.579
It is an unusual work for the renowned inventor
and artist due to the compilation’s laser

00:10:55.579 --> 00:11:00.209
focus on a single topic: the study of water.

00:11:00.209 --> 00:11:05.059
Among the pages are sheets of calculations
and musings on everything from water currents

00:11:05.059 --> 00:11:11.170
and whirlpools—to practical uses for water
in structures like canals, locks, and dams.

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:17.559
Admittedly a bit of a fanboy, Mr. Gates finds
value in the works of Da Vinci “because

00:11:17.559 --> 00:11:23.490
he personally worked out science on his own”,
also noting that “every one of Da Vinci’s

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:28.389
notebooks are amazing documents—they’re
kind of his rough-draft notes of texts that

00:11:28.389 --> 00:11:30.910
he eventually wanted to put together”.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:35.540
So save your doodles and scraps, because someday
a billionaire might be willing to pay top

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.250
dollar for them!

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:42.429
When you consider that water is essential
for human life, it seems rather difficult

00:11:42.429 --> 00:11:44.399
to put a price on it.

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:48.779
Of course, water is a common paid utility
in many countries, where people can also enjoy

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:54.110
it by the bottle for convenience—but the
existential question remains.

00:11:54.110 --> 00:12:00.420
The California based Beverly Hills Drink Company
seems to have made peace with the issue—going

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:06.670
so far as to offer the self-proclaimed “most
expensive bottle of water in the world”

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.959
for an affordable $100 thousand dollars.

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:16.079
Only nine bottles of their Luxury Collection,
Diamond Edition bottles were made, rendering

00:12:16.079 --> 00:12:19.279
the experience as exclusive as you might expect.

00:12:19.279 --> 00:12:23.850
The bottles were designed by jeweler Mario
Padilla to be the perfect complement to the

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:30.000
unique thirst quenching experience, each one
sealed with a cap made of white gold, set

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.629
with over 600 white diamonds and 250 black
diamonds for a total of 14 carats worth of

00:12:37.629 --> 00:12:38.629
gems.

00:12:38.629 --> 00:12:44.209
A special case filled with four engraved Baccarat
crystal tumblers completes the package, and

00:12:44.209 --> 00:12:49.309
its all accompanied by a personal private
water tasting presentation anywhere in the

00:12:49.309 --> 00:12:54.699
world by water sommelier (yes that’s actually
a job) Martin Riese.

00:12:54.699 --> 00:12:58.839
Whatever you have a passion for, there’s
a way to make a living doing it.

00:12:58.839 --> 00:13:00.819
Even if that passion is water tasting.

00:13:00.819 --> 00:13:01.819
Truly inspirational.

00:13:01.819 --> 00:13:08.389
After a taste test of the $100 thousand water
by American musicians 2 Chainz and Diplo,

00:13:08.389 --> 00:13:12.130
they reported that the water “tastes like
fruit”.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:18.970
Despite their “acquired expensive taste”,
after the tasting 2 Chainz sagely noted that

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:24.389
“if you put a diamond on something, they’re
gonna run up the tab on you” and that “neither

00:13:24.389 --> 00:13:29.249
one of us believe that we should pay $100
thousand for a bottle of water”.

00:13:29.249 --> 00:13:34.309
But if you choose to investigate the Diamond
Collection for yourself, be sure to take advantage

00:13:34.309 --> 00:13:37.329
of the one year supply of free water refills.

00:13:37.329 --> 00:13:41.170
Make the most of that diamond encrusted bottle.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:46.679
Very few artists are lucky enough to see their
work appreciated on a grand scale, and 19th

00:13:46.679 --> 00:13:50.029
century painter Paul Gauguin is no exception.

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:54.069
It would certainly come as a surprise to him
that one of his works would eventually become

00:13:54.069 --> 00:14:00.779
the priciest painting in the world, fetching
$300 million in a secretive auction over a

00:14:00.779 --> 00:14:04.110
century after he first set color to canvas.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:10.910
The painting—titled When Will You Marry?—depicts
a snapshot of Tahitian life in the late 1800s,

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:15.999
and was the result of Gauguin’s sabbatical
on the Polynesian island in search of “an

00:14:15.999 --> 00:14:19.620
edenic paradise” to inspire “pure, ‘primitive’
art”.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:26.649
It was sold by a Swiss private collection
in February of 2015 to an enigmatic buyer

00:14:26.649 --> 00:14:30.170
who chose not to reveal their identity publically
during the sale.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:35.600
Rudolf Staechelin, a former executive with
the luxury auction house Sotheby’s, confirmed

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:40.439
the sale which was made via his family trust,
with the Gauguin being only one of at least

00:14:40.439 --> 00:14:45.949
twenty other magnificent works of art in his
possession from the Impressionist and Post-Impressionist

00:14:45.949 --> 00:14:46.949
era.

00:14:46.949 --> 00:14:51.110
He seemingly suffered from a rare case of
having so much priceless artwork that he truly

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.540
didn’t know what to do with it all.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.230
Before the auction, Staechelin had been loaning
“When Will You Marry?”

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:03.459
to the Kunstmuseum in Switzerland along with
other pieces of art from his private collection.

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:09.600
When asked what prompted the sale he replied,
quite simply, that “we got a good offer”.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:14.410
Unfortunately for the Swiss museum curators,
they will have to hope for another philanthropic

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:18.519
donation to fill the newly emptied exhibit.

00:15:18.519 --> 00:15:21.950
Smartphones have become an integral part of
our cultural identity.

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:27.459
Equal parts tools and status symbols—the
brands we choose and the accessories we decorate

00:15:27.459 --> 00:15:30.920
them with can reveal quite a lot about a person.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:36.319
One Chinese businessman decided that the options
available at his local electronics store simply

00:15:36.319 --> 00:15:38.139
didn’t cut it for him.

00:15:38.139 --> 00:15:39.420
So what did he do?

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:44.480
Why, customize an iPhone 5 with a 26-carat
black diamond and a body made of gold, of

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.480
course!

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:51.059
And all told, it costs a staggering $15.3
million dollars—the most expensive smartphone

00:15:51.059 --> 00:15:52.570
out there today.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:58.639
The phone was the result of a request sent
to UK based craftsman Stuart Hughes, who specializes

00:15:58.639 --> 00:16:01.420
in high-end luxury smartphones.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:09.610
It took nine weeks to assemble, and includes
135 grams of 24-carat gold and 653 diamonds

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:10.880
in the design.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:15.480
The giant black diamond at the heart of it
all—seated where the Home button normally

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:19.970
resides—was a family heirloom passed down
through the anonymous businessman’s family

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:21.749
for generations.

00:16:21.749 --> 00:16:25.861
Rather than build a display case for it to
set upon the mantle, he chose to have use

00:16:25.861 --> 00:16:31.329
it as his everyday phone, giving him the opportunity
to show it off at every corporate meeting

00:16:31.329 --> 00:16:33.379
and business lunch.

00:16:33.379 --> 00:16:38.160
Before the black diamond iPhone crossed his
desk, Hughes had worked up an iPhone 4S made

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.309
of gold that was valued at six million GBP.

00:16:42.309 --> 00:16:47.170
It held the world record for most expensive
smartphone only briefly, before this one came

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:50.259
along and smashed that record to pieces.

00:16:50.259 --> 00:16:54.359
Whether the black diamond iPhone will be a
decision that the businessman comes to regret,

00:16:54.359 --> 00:16:59.339
who can say—but if there’s one thing for
certain, it’s that a good smartphone is

00:16:59.339 --> 00:17:02.969
indeed worth its weight in gold.

